.TH MACME 1
.SH NAME
macme, apdf, aimg, aweb \- acme with image windows
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B macme
[
.I acme options
]
.PP
.B apdf
[
.B -r
.I dpi
]
.I file
[
.I page
]
.PP
.B aimg
[
.B -s
.I percent
]
.I file
.PP
.B aweb
[
.B -h
.I height
]
.I url
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Macme
is
.IR acme (1)
with one addition, as a prototype:
the body of a window can show an image instead of text.
Everything else, including its file system
(it is posted as
.B acme
too, so
.IR win ,
.IR 9p (1)
and other clients work unchanged), is the same as in
.IR acme .
.PP
Each window directory has a file
.BR image .
An
.IR image (6),
compressed or not, written to it is shown in the body
when the file is closed, replacing any previous one.
Reading it gives four numbers: the width and height of the
part of the body that shows the image, and those of the image,
so that clients can fit the image to the window.
The ctl message
.B noimage
goes back to showing text.
The ctl message
.B pagekeys
makes the PgUp, PgDn, Home and End keys come as
.B KX
events, as if
.BR pgup ,
.BR pgdown ,
.B home
or
.B end
had been executed, instead of scrolling.
.PP
The wheel, the scroll bar, and the arrow, page, Home and End keys
scroll the image; typing does not change it.
On macOS, horizontal swipes (e.g., on a Magic Mouse) scroll it
sideways: macme sets
.B $hwheel
when it starts
.IR devdraw ,
which then sends them as mouse events with buttons 8 and 16
both set, button 1 set for left, and the amount shifted left 5 bits.
Clicking on the image sends an event
.IP
.EX
MI\fIx y\fP 0 1 \fIb\fP
.EE
.PP
to the window's
.B event
file, with
.I x
and
.I y
the position in the image and
.I b
the buttons (1, 2, 4), as the event text, so that
.IR acmeevent (1)
reports it as the text.
.PP
.I Apdf
shows a page of a PDF
.I file
(the first by default) in a new window,
using
.IR mutool ,
.IR pdftoppm ,
or
.IR gs ,
whichever is found, and
.IR png (1)
to make the image.
.B Next
and
.B Prev
in the tag, or the PgDn and PgUp keys, move through the pages;
Home and End go to the first and last ones.
.B Zoom+
and
.B Zoom-
change their size, and
.B Zoom!
fits the page to the window.
.PP
.I Aimg
shows an image file (PNG, JPEG and GIF with the
.IR jpg (1)
programs, others with ImageMagick's
.IR convert ),
with the same
.BR Zoom+ ,
.B Zoom-
and
.B Zoom!
in the tag.
.PP
.I Aweb
shows a web page (a file name or a URL) rendered as an image,
as wide as the window and
.I height
pixels tall (4000 by default), with a headless Chrome or Chromium
(or
.B $browser
if set), or Firefox.
.B Links
opens a window with the links in the page, to be plumbed;
clicking on a link with button 1 or 3 follows it, and button 2
opens it in a new window (the link boxes come from running a
script in the page; without Chrome, links are not clickable);
button 2 on a URL in the tag shows it;
.B Back
goes back,
.B Reload
renders again,
.B More
doubles the height, and
.B Zoom+
and
.B Zoom-
scale the page.
.PP
All of them write
.B dumpdir
and
.B dump
to the window's
.B ctl
file (see
.IR acme (4)),
with the page, resolution or scale in use, so
.B Dump
and
.B Load
(or a restart with
.BR -l )
bring their windows back as they were, in the same
column and place.
Other clients should do the same, as
.I win
does.
.SH EXAMPLE
Show an image computed by a program:
.IP
.EX
\&. $PLAN9/lib/acme.rc
newwindow
winname /tmp/plot
plot ... | topng | png -t | winwrite image
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B \*9/src/cmd/macme
.br
.B \*9/bin/apdf
.br
.B \*9/bin/aimg
.br
.B \*9/bin/aweb
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR acme (1),
.IR acme (4),
.IR acmeevent (1),
.IR jpg (1),
.IR image (6)
.SH BUGS
It is a prototype: the image is kept in memory, the body text
is hidden but kept, and there is no way to zoom in macme itself.
.I Apdf
needs
.IR pdfinfo ,
.IR mutool ,
or
.I gs
to find the last page for End.
